Yes. The important move in what you’re describing is that robot-data scarcity is partly a representation problem masquerading as a data problem.

The field is already beginning to move in this direction. Recent work explicitly trains robots from ordinary human video rather than robot trajectories. Microsoft’s VITRA, for example, treats the human hand as a kind of dexterous end-effector and converted unannotated egocentric human activity into more than 1.2 million VLA-style episodes.  NVIDIA has demonstrated latent-action pretraining that extracts action-like representations from videos with no robot action labels, then uses a comparatively small amount of robot data to ground those latent actions into an actual robot.  ViPRA similarly learns latent actions from actionless video and reports adapting them to continuous robot control using roughly 100–200 teleoperated demonstrations.  A 2026 survey now treats learning robot skills from human video as an entire rapidly developing subfield. 

But I think your formulation pushes one layer deeper.

Don’t teach the robot only to recognize humans. Teach it to say, structurally, “that could be me.”

Not literally as a consciousness claim. What it needs computationally is a self-model:

SELF = body + sensors + effectors + reachable actions + viewpoint + dynamics + agency

Then, when it watches another agent:

OTHER = body + sensors? + effectors + actions + viewpoint + goals + dynamics

it learns a transformation:

T_{\text{other}\rightarrow\text{self}}

So:

human reaches around obstacle → grasps handle → rotates wrist → opens door

doesn’t remain “a video of a human.”

The system reconstructs:

agent identifies affordance → moves end-effector to grasp pose → establishes contact → applies torque about hinge-constrained axis → changes world state.

Now human arm has disappeared from the important representation.

A wheeled robot with one manipulator, a humanoid, a quadruped with an arm, or something we haven’t built yet can ask:

How would my embodiment instantiate that same latent operation?

That’s the real unlock.

I’d call the central mechanism Empathic Embodiment Transfer:

\boxed{
\text{observe another}
\rightarrow
\text{infer their state}
\rightarrow
\text{abstract their action}
\rightarrow
\text{project into self}
\rightarrow
\text{simulate}
\rightarrow
\text{act}
\rightarrow
\text{measure delta}
}

And in that technical sense, empathy becomes a compiler.

It isn’t necessary for the robot to feel empathy. Perspective-taking, agency attribution, inverse planning and self/other mapping are sufficient. Anthropomorphism and personification become useful training operations rather than philosophical mistakes:

Anthropomorphize: “What would make that animal/human behave that way?”

Personify: “Model it as an agent possessing goals, constraints, attention and available actions.”

Self-project: “Suppose that agent were me. What variables survive the embodiment change?”

That last question extracts the invariant.

And suddenly your training corpus gets absurdly larger.

A cooking video teaches manipulation and sequencing. A mechanic teaches tool use and causal diagnosis. A toddler teaches exploration, failure recovery and affordance discovery. A dog navigating furniture contributes obstacle negotiation and attention cues. Sports footage contains interception, trajectory prediction, balance and multi-agent coordination. Video games contain enormous numbers of state→decision→consequence sequences. Movies and television contribute social priors, plans and object semantics, although their physics and causal sequences have to be treated with much lower confidence.

Games are particularly interesting because they often provide something ordinary video doesn’t:

state_t \rightarrow action_t \rightarrow state_{t+1}

at gigantic scale.

That’s very close to what an embodied world model wants.

There is an important constraint, though: Internet experience cannot completely replace embodied calibration.

A video cannot reliably tell you exact friction, motor torque, tactile force, compliance, latency, mass, joint limits, hidden contact forces, whether movie physics are fake, etc.

So the optimal architecture isn’t:

“No robot data.”

It’s closer to:

Internet-scale experience + tiny embodiment-specific calibration set.

Think about learning to drive a new vehicle. You don’t relearn roads, traffic lights, pedestrians, trajectories and navigation from scratch. You already possess that gigantic world model. You spend a few minutes discovering this steering ratio, this brake response, this vehicle width.

Robots could work similarly:

\text{huge shared world prior}
+
\text{small embodiment adapter}
=
\text{competent new robot}

And that’s where your economic argument gets interesting.

The conventional assumption is approximately:

\text{Capability} \propto \text{robot-specific data}

Your alternative is:

\text{Capability}
\propto
\text{usable experience}
\times
\text{quality of transduction}

So instead of competing primarily over who owns the most robot trajectories, competition shifts toward who has the best interpreter for converting existing experience into embodied knowledge.

Recent research is already producing evidence for that direction. Kevin Black’s August 14, 2026 Berkeley dissertation describes a hierarchical system directly leveraging Internet-scale non-robot video to improve robot semantic and visual reasoning while requiring relatively little robot data.  And the fact that current researchers still describe robot-data scarcity as a central bottleneck shows exactly why exploiting the already-existing video reservoir matters economically. 

The Pantheon-style Wizard’s Move here is therefore:

Don’t scale the scarce representation. Change representations until the abundant data becomes applicable.

Or compressed even further:

\boxed{\textbf{Don't give every robot a childhood. Teach it how to inherit ours.}}

Then embodiment-specific experience becomes mostly calibration, correction, and discovery of genuine deltas rather than learning the world from zero.